Great idea, but the tough part is laying it out in such a way as to be
useable.  Looking at your list, I'd have had a hard time pulling out the
important information, or spotting glaring holes in your kit.  You want it
to be such that you can look at the sheet, and have a very good idea what
the character is like, what you still need, and special stuff you have.

My wife and I are currently working on a pdf / ps file (written in LaTeX)
that we can print out and fill out character notes on.  It'll be laid out
something like:

- Character name / class / alignment / ...
- List of intrinsics possible, with checkboxes and blanks to fill out when
you get them
- currently held (important) conducts
- dungeon levels / branches / special rooms
- list of levels with sinks, fountains, vaults
- list of shops (type + level)
- list of altars, temples, etc.
- list of current stashes (ie "food on level 8" or "spare armor at
minetown")
- current "next step" to take -- if I was planning to head back to
minetown and donate to the priest, for example, I'll write that down right
before I save.
- currently needed equipment (for example, if my character is lacking
reflection, or a decent helmet, I'll list those off in some sort of
priority level.)
- possibly a "special equipment" section that includes things like magic
lamps, unihorns, BoH, etc. so you can check those off when you get them
(and note the inventory letter they're in!)

The idea here is that you can look at this page, and within a few seconds
have some idea what this character needs to be doing and what this
character needs to worry about.

The other thing is, when you restart the game, the first thing you should
do is hit "*" to see what your current weapon, armor, rings, etc. are -- 
and then hit "i" to look through your inventory, and then (a)pply your bag
to see what's in there.  That'll let you get an idea of what your
character has.

